    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Introduction",
        "text": "Kazuhiro Tsuga is a Japanese business executive who is serving as chairman of Panasonic ."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Early life and education",
        "text": "Tsuga was born in Japan in 1956 and studied at Osaka University and graduated in 1979 with a B.S. in bioengineering.\nHe immediately joined the workforce, and obtained an M.S. in computer science from the University of California, Santa Barbara in, 1986 through a company sponsorship."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Panasonic",
        "text": "Tsuga first joined Panasonic , then named Matsushita Electric, in 1979. With a background in research and development, Tsuga's first executive management position came in 2008.\nHe was elevated to President of Panasonic on June 27, 2012, taking over for Fumio Ōtsubo . Otsubo, before being replaced, racked up losses of 750 billion yen.\nTsuga focused on returning Panasonic to profitability, highlighted by cutting 10% of the workforce in addition to re-prioritizing Panasonic's business strategy."
      },
      {
        "heading": "External links",
        "text": "Panasonic biography Archived 2014-10-03 at the Wayback Machine"
      }
    ],
    "full_text": "Introduction\nKazuhiro Tsuga is a Japanese business executive who is serving as chairman of Panasonic .\n\nEarly life and education\nTsuga was born in Japan in 1956 and studied at Osaka University and graduated in 1979 with a B.S. in bioengineering.\nHe immediately joined the workforce, and obtained an M.S. in computer science from the University of California, Santa Barbara in, 1986 through a company sponsorship.\n\nPanasonic\nTsuga first joined Panasonic , then named Matsushita Electric, in 1979. With a background in research and development, Tsuga's first executive management position came in 2008.\nHe was elevated to President of Panasonic on June 27, 2012, taking over for Fumio Ōtsubo . Otsubo, before being replaced, racked up losses of 750 billion yen.\nTsuga focused on returning Panasonic to profitability, highlighted by cutting 10% of the workforce in addition to re-prioritizing Panasonic's business strategy.\n\nExternal links\nPanasonic biography Archived 2014-10-03 at the Wayback Machine"
  },
